ScopeDue is source-available. It is NOT open source, and this is not an open source license.
The code is published for one reason: so that anyone can check for themselves how ScopeDue
handles their data (what it keeps on the device, what it sends, and where).


1. What you may do

   a. Read the code, and review, test and audit it, alone or with tools (including AI tools).
   b. Install the published ScopeDue extension, or run this code unchanged on your own computer,
      to check how it behaves.
   c. Write about what you find, and quote short parts of the code to show it.
   d. Report security problems to the owner (see SECURITY-AUDIT.md), and suggest fixes to them.


2. What you may not do without the owner's written permission

   a. Copy, fork, clone, mirror or republish this code, in whole or in part, other than the copy
      a hosting site makes on its own when you view it.
   b. Change it and use or share the changed version (a derivative work), or port or rewrite it
      into another language, framework or product.
   c. Sell, license, sublicense, rent or give it away, or ship it inside another product.
   d. Run it, or anything made from it, as a service for other people, or use it to build or
      improve a product or service that competes with ScopeDue.
   e. Publish it, or anything made from it, under another name, logo or brand ("rebranding"), or
      present it as your own work.
   f. Use it as material to generate a copy, clone or close imitation of ScopeDue, including by
      asking an AI model or agent to do so.
   g. Remove or change this license, or the notices at the top of the files.


